: \nL2: \ue200cite\ue2020\u2020Skip to main content\ue201 \ue200cite\ue2021\u2020Main Navigation\ue201 L3: \nL4: \ue200cite\ue2022\u2020UBS home\ue201 L5: \nL6: \ue200cite\ue2023\u2020Chief Investment Office\ue201 L7: \nL8: ## Additional language and service options\nL9: \nL10:   * \ue200cite\ue2024\u2020Locations\ue201 L11: \nL12:   * Client logins\ue200cite\ue2025\u2020Client logins\ue201 L13: ### Choose your login\nL14: \nL15: \ue200cite\ue2025\u2020More logins\ue201 L16: \nL17:     * #### Individuals\nL18: \nL19:       * \ue200cite\ue2026\u2020UBS E-Banking Switzerland\u2020ebanking-ch.ubs.com\ue201 L20:       * \ue200cite\ue2027\u2020UBS Quotes\ue201 L21:       * \ue200cite\ue2028\u2020UBS Safe\u2020safe-ch.ubs.com\ue201 L22:       * \ue200cite\ue2029\u2020US client account login\u2020onlineservices.ubs.com\ue201 L23:       * \ue200cite\ue20210\u2020UBS Digital Networks and Events\u2020my.ubs.com\ue201 L24: \nL25:     * #### Corporate & Institutions\nL26:       * \ue200cite\ue20211\u2020UBS E-Banking and online services Switzerland\u2020ebanking-ch1.ubs.com\ue201 L27:       * \ue200cite\ue20212\u2020UBS Connect\u2020connect-ch.ubs.com\ue201 L28:       * \ue200cite\ue20213\u2020UBS Neo\u2020neo.ubs.com\ue201 L29:       * \ue200cite\ue20214\u2020UBS Real Estate US\u2020neo.ubs.com\ue201 L30:       * \ue200cite\ue20215\u2020UBS Asset Wizard\u2020asset-wizard.ubs.com\ue201 L31:       * \ue200cite\ue20216\u2020Asset Management Client Portal\u2020neo.ubs.com\ue201 L32: \nL33: \ue200cite\ue2025\u2020Client logins\ue201 L34: \nL35: \ue200cite\ue20217\u2020Image: header.search.alt\ue201 L36: \nL37: header.search.error\nL38: ### Search Title\nL39: \nL40: Close\nL41: \nL42: Open the menu\nL43: \nL44: # Brent tops 100: A milestone, not a turning point\nL45: \nL46: CIO Daily Updates\nL47: \nL48: by\u00a0Chief Investment Office 09 Sep 2026 4\u00a0min read\nL49: \nL50: ##\nL51: \nL52: From the studio\nL53: \nL54: Video:\ue200cite\ue20218\u2020Market Playbook | What do rate hikes mean for portfolios?\ue201 (5 mins)\nL55: Video: \ue200cite\ue20219\u2020Four reasons to take a closer look at Taiwanese equities\ue201 (5 mins)\nL56: ##\nL57: \nL58: Thought of the day\nL59: Brent crude has climbed back above USD 100 a barrel, its highest level since late July, as US-Iran military exchanges continue to threaten energy supplies. US forces said they destroyed five Iranian oil tankers following attempted attacks on a US Navy ship, while Iran reported retaliatory strikes on a US base in Jordan. Houthi missile and drone strikes caused fires at Saudi energy facilities this week, with officials reporting temporary operational halts.\nL60: Washington also announced further economic sanctions targeting Iran\u2019s aviation sector.\nL61: The surge in crude prices is consistent with tightening physical supply. Oil inventories stored at sea have fallen by 150 million barrels over two months, while Gulf exports remain below pre-conflict levels. Chinese crude imports recovered to nearly 9 million barrels per day in August, from 7.15 million in June, suggesting demand is firming despite higher prices.\nL62: Against this backdrop, we have raised our Brent forecasts to USD 95/bbl for year-end and USD 90/bbl for March 2027, from USD 85/bbl and USD 80/bbl, respectively. Although below the current spot price, both forecasts remain above corresponding futures prices.\nL63: The rise above USD 100/bbl could prove psychologically important for markets. But we believe earnings growth and structural investment remain the more important drivers for the investment outlook:\nL64: Earnings, AI investment can withstand higher energy price. We expect US earnings growth of 25% in 2026 and 14% in 2027, providing a strong foundation despite higher energy costs. Recent results from NVIDIA and Broadcom\u2014whose managements guided for strong revenue growth into 2027\u2014offer further evidence of sustained AI infrastructure demand.\nL65: The earnings recovery also extends beyond technology and the US: We recently raised our European earnings growth forecast to 15% for 2026 and continue to expect 15% growth in 2027.\n"